"""Build student-readable notebooks 02–06; execution is a separate explicit step."""
from pathlib import Path
import sys
import nbformat as nbf

ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
SAVE_NUMBERS=set(sys.argv[1:] or ['02','03','04','05','06'])
NAMES={'02':'duplicate_target_ambiguity_and_leakage_investigation','03':'training_eda_and_preprocessing','04':'feature_engineering','05':'feature_selection','06':'baseline_models'}

def notebook(number,title,introduction):
    cells=[nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell(f'# {number}. {title}\n\n{introduction}\n\nThis revision preserves the earlier work in `snapshots/workflow_20261012T143405Z`. Earlier generated artifacts remain in place. New evidence is saved under `revision_v2`. Read `docs/revision_v2/research_alignment.md` for the reference-paper and data gaps.'),
           nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell('## 1. Check our starting point\n\n**Question:** Are these the same source files and frozen rows?\n\n**Why it matters:** A comparison is fair only when its data, labels and row order are fixed.\n\n**Method:** Check protected SHA-256 hashes (fingerprints of file bytes), source targets and the saved role links. `train` is the fitting role; `model_selection` is for comparing candidates; `calibration` is reserved for later checking whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies. Calibration is not used to fit or choose anything here.'),
           nbf.v4.new_code_cell(f'''from pathlib import Path
import sys
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from IPython.display import display, Markdown, Image
ROOT = next(p for p in (Path.cwd(), *Path.cwd().parents) if (p / "src").is_dir() and (p / "data/raw").is_dir())
sys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT))
if sys.prefix == sys.base_prefix:
    raise RuntimeError("Use the project .venv kernel.")
from src.research_revision.common import start, finish, show_prior, explain, table
from src.research_revision import data_audits as audit
s = start(ROOT, "{number}")
display(pd.read_csv(s['out']['tables'] / "{number}_role_class_support.csv"))''')]
    return cells

def md(c,text): c.append(nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell(text))
def code(c,text): c.append(nbf.v4.new_code_cell(text))

def save(number,cells):
    if number not in SAVE_NUMBERS: return
    nb=nbf.v4.new_notebook(cells=cells,metadata=dict(kernelspec=dict(display_name='Botnet research (.venv)',language='python',name='botnet-research'),language_info=dict(name='python',version='3.11.9'),research_revision='v2'))
    path=ROOT/f'notebooks/{number}_{NAMES[number]}.ipynb'
    # Resolve the established filename, including Notebook 03's existing spelling.
    paths=list((ROOT/'notebooks').glob(number+'_*.ipynb'))
    if len(paths)==1: path=paths[0]
    nbf.write(nb,path); print(path.name,len(cells),'cells')

c=notebook('02','Data problems and a fair evaluation','**Our question:** What can repeated records, conflicting labels and train/test overlap do to our conclusions?\n\n**Inputs:** The two supplied CSVs, Notebook 01 audits and frozen development assignments. We repeat the previously disclosed official-test audit here, solely to verify history.\n\n**Expected result:** A defensible evaluation protocol—not cleaned labels, models or a new split.')
md(c,'## 2. Reproduce the first audit\n\n**Question:** Can we reproduce Notebook 01 instead of trusting a written summary?\n\n**Why:** A wrong starting count would affect every later result.\n\n**Method:** Load the supplied files separately; repeat missing-value, duplicate and integrity checks. Recompute exact original-pattern groups, excluding ID and both targets. The official test was already inspected in Notebooks 01–02; this access repeats that diagnostic history, and will not inform feature or model choices.')
code(c,'audit.audit_original(s)')
md(c,'## 3. Define the prediction task\n\nWe predict ten `attack_cat` classes, including Normal. Binary `label` has 0 for Normal and 1 for Attack; it is a second view of the same records. **Attack does not mean verified Botnet.** No literal Botnet class appears in these files.\n\nThe 42 predictors exclude `id`, `label` and `attack_cat`. Later row positions, group IDs and role names are bookkeeping, never inputs. Feature definitions describe intended measurements, not verified extractor behaviour. No raw DNS query names/types, endpoints or timestamps are available to establish a DNS stream or a time sequence.')
code(c,"display(pd.DataFrame([dict(dataset=r,rows=len(df),classes=df.attack_cat.nunique(),literal_botnet_rows=int(df.attack_cat.str.lower().eq('botnet').sum()),binary_disagreements=int(df.label.ne(df.attack_cat.ne('Normal').astype(int)).sum())) for r,df in s['datasets'].items()]))\nprint('Original predictors:', ', '.join(s['original']))")
md(c,'## 4. Understand what a duplicate means\n\n**Question:** How many rows have exactly the same 42 measurements?\n\n**Why:** Row counts can exaggerate the amount of distinct evidence.\n\n**Method:** Full exact tuples define patterns; no rounding, target or ID participates. A group with three rows has **three participating rows** but only **two excess duplicate rows**. We examine group sizes and class composition. Identical recorded measurements need not be the same physical event.')
code(c,"toy = pd.DataFrame({'bytes':[100,100,100,200], 'label':[0,0,1,1]})\ntoy['feature_group'] = pd.factorize(toy['bytes'])[0]\ndisplay(toy)\nprint('2 vectors; 3 participating repeated rows; 2 excess rows. The conflicting third row stays in the same group.')")
code(c,'audit.duplicates(s)')
md(c,'## 5. Check conflicting targets\n\n**Question:** Can one input pattern have incompatible labels?\n\n**Why:** A deterministic model gives one prediction for identical inputs. Missing context or label ambiguity may prevent perfect classification from these measurements.\n\n**Method:** First form predictor groups, then inspect their labels. Binary conflicts and category conflicts are counted separately. The heatmap counts groups sharing each unordered class pair, not all possible row pairs. A group with three classes contributes to three pairs, so pair totals are not a count of unique groups.')
code(c,'audit.conflicts(s)')
md(c,'## 6. Confirm train/test overlap\n\n**Question:** Does the official test contain patterns already in the supplied training file?\n\n**Why:** Such rows may measure recognition of recorded patterns rather than generalisation to unseen patterns.\n\n**Method:** Full tuple equality confirms dictionary-hash candidates. “Consistent” means the union of labels has one class; “conflicting” means multiple classes, even if both files have the same multi-label set. Count vectors and participating rows separately; define overlap masks but retain the complete official test.')
code(c,'audit.cross_split(s)')
md(c,'## 7. Check meanings and possible leakage\n\n**Question:** Would each feature exist when a prediction is made?\n\n**Why:** A highly predictive measurement is useful only if it is available in the intended setting.\n\n**Method:** Plot the recorded classes against ID and repeat special-value checks. Examine contextual connection counts (`ct_*`), protocol markers, FTP values and extraction assumptions. Leakage means information is improperly available to a model; correlation with a label alone cannot prove it. ID ordering is an audit observation, not a timestamp.')
code(c,'audit.semantics(s)')
md(c,'## 8. Keep records and frozen roles\n\n**Question:** Which policy preserves evidence without letting identical inputs cross development roles?\n\n**Why:** Deleting conflicts or majority-relabeling would silently change our target problem.\n\n**Method:** Keep every original row and label. Quantify rejected deletion alternatives without applying them. Verify the already frozen assignment: whole exact predictor groups stay in one role, including all conflicting versions. Targets help the original stratification, but never define groups. We do not redraw the assignment for better scores.')
code(c,'audit.policy_and_roles(s)')
md(c,'## 9. Freeze the evaluation rules\n\n**Question:** What does “better detection” mean?\n\n**Why:** Accuracy can hide failure on rare classes.\n\n**Method and decision:** Use fixed-ten-class **macro F1** as the primary measure: compute F1 for each of the ten classes, then average equally. F1 balances precision (how many predicted cases are correct) and recall (how many actual cases are found). Also report accuracy, weighted F1, per-class precision/recall/F1/support, and raw/row-normalized confusion matrices. Support means the number of actual rows.\n\nFor Attack/Normal, derive Attack from a multiclass prediction other than Normal. Report attack recall, attack precision and **false-positive rate**: Normal rows wrongly called Attack divided by all Normal rows. Where probabilities exist, use `1 − P(Normal)` for precision–recall curves and average precision; no operating threshold is chosen here.\n\nGrouped inner folds inside `train` may tune candidates with fold-fitted preprocessing. `model_selection` compares the final fitting-only candidates; it is already exposed to ten earlier experiments and is not unbiased final evidence. `calibration` is for later confidence calibration, distinct from model training and threshold selection. The final official test stays separate. Later report full-test scores plus previously defined overlap/non-overlap diagnostics; never train or choose settings on its outcomes. Exact-vector separation does not prove host or temporal independence.')
code(c,"protocol = pd.DataFrame([('train','Fit preprocessing/models; grouped internal tuning'),('model_selection','Compare candidates; disclose prior exposure'),('calibration','Reserved for later probability calibration'),('official test','Reserved final evaluation; historical audit only')], columns=['population','purpose'])\ndisplay(table(s,protocol,'evaluation_roles'))")
code(c,"finish(s, findings='Critical counts reproduce. Training has 101,040 patterns and 1,772 category-conflicting groups. The official splits share 1,302 vectors. ID ordering and special values need careful interpretation.', decisions='Keep all original rows and labels. Preserve the frozen group-disjoint development roles. Use ten-class macro F1 and class-level diagnostics; preserve the complete official test.', limitations='Distinct recorded vectors do not establish distinct events or host/time independence. Flow-extractor causality and botnet/DNS/temporal ground truth are unresolved. The test was historically audited; model-selection rows have prior experiment exposure.', next_question='What does fitting-only EDA justify about numeric and categorical preprocessing?')")
save('02',c)

c=notebook('03','Understand fitting data and prepare model inputs','**Our question:** Which transparent transformations make these flow measurements usable without changing their meaning or leaking held-out information?\n\n**Inputs:** Frozen roles, original 42 predictors and protected earlier preprocessing definitions. Only fitting rows inform EDA and fitting.\n\n**Expected result:** Reproduced raw-numeric tree inputs and scaled inputs, with explanations and verified row links. No models or feature selection.')
md(c,'## 2. See class support, ranges and categories\n\n**Question:** Which fitting patterns do we actually have?\n\n**Why:** Rare classes, zeros and long numeric tails affect what a model can learn.\n\n**Method:** Count both rows and distinct original patterns. Examine every numeric column’s range, median, 99th percentile, maximum, zeros, skewness and number of values. Skewness describes an uneven tail; it is not a rule for deleting rows. Count categorical values and their pattern support. All these summaries use `train` only.')
code(c,'audit.fit_eda(s)')
md(c,'## 3. Look at protocol and target relationships\n\n**Question:** Are zeros and differences plausible flow behaviour?\n\n**Why:** A zero response size can mean no observed response, not missing data. TCP-specific timing need not behave like UDP.\n\n**Method:** Compare zero frequencies by protocol and selected distributions by class. Save row/pattern support and pattern-weighted means. A pattern-weighted mean gives one original group total weight one, retaining all conflicting labels. This is a diagnostic view, not a resampling rule or training weight.')
code(c,'audit.relationships(s)')
md(c,'## 4. Keep the full fitting EDA\n\n**Question:** Do the broad views agree with the selected summaries?\n\n**Why:** Selected plots alone can hide unusual columns or relationships.\n\n**Method:** The following protected earlier figures cover all numeric distributions, correlation, ECDFs and selected feature pairs. They were made from these same hash-verified fitting rows. We retain them as reviewed evidence rather than pretend that every visualization was newly invented. An ECDF shows the fraction of values at or below a point; a correlation summarizes association, not causal effect.')
code(c,"for filename in ['03_numeric_histograms_fit.png','03_numeric_boxplot_fit.png','03_numeric_ecdf_fit.png','03_numeric_correlation_fit.png','03_selected_feature_pairs_fit.png']:\n    display(Image(filename=str(ROOT/'results/figures'/filename)))")
md(c,'## 5. State the preprocessing choices\n\n**Question:** What changes, and what stays as recorded?\n\n**Why:** Each transformation must have a specific purpose.\n\n**Decision:** Keep `service="-"` as its own observed category. Keep nonbinary FTP counts as numeric values and both FTP columns. Preserve zero values and extremes. There are no missing predictors, so no imputation is fitted. No clipping, feature deletion, resampling or SMOTE is used.\n\nTree inputs keep all numeric values in their original units. The scaled representation applies `log1p(x) = log(1+x)` to 13 previously fitting-defined nonnegative, long-tail quantities, then robust scaling. Other numeric columns receive robust scaling directly. Robust scaling subtracts the fitting median and divides by the fitting interquartile range (75th minus 25th percentile). It changes units, not labels or rank order. Both representations one-hot encode the whole `proto`, `service`, and `state` categories. Unseen categories have an all-zero block; this is documented, not silently merged into Normal.')
code(c,"show_prior(s,'03_preprocessing_decisions')\nexample = pd.DataFrame({'bytes':[0,10,100,1000,10000]})\nexample['log1p_bytes'] = np.log1p(example.bytes)\nmedian = example.log1p_bytes.median(); iqr = example.log1p_bytes.quantile(.75)-example.log1p_bytes.quantile(.25)\nexample['robust_scaled_log'] = (example.log1p_bytes-median)/iqr\ndisplay(table(s,example,'scaling_worked_example'))\nprint('Toy illustration only; real scalers fit only on the frozen fitting rows.')")
md(c,'## 6. Refit only on fitting rows and verify\n\n**Question:** Do the actual transformations reproduce the expected inputs?\n\n**Why:** A correct written choice is insufficient if row order, names or learned state differ.\n\n**Method:** Clone the protected preprocessing definitions, fit them freshly on `train`, transform each existing role, and compare every sparse value with the earlier matrices. Save/reload the new pipelines and matrices. Check names, shapes, finite values and exact role overlap. Count unknown categories by role for integrity; held-out outcomes do not change the fitting vocabulary. The before/after plots show actual transformed quantities. Raw panels display their central 99% for readability; all values remain in the inputs.')
code(c,'audit.preprocessing(s)')
md(c,'## 7. What these inputs can support\n\nTrees can split raw counters and categories. Linear models need comparable numeric scales and explicit one-hot groups. Later neural comparisons should share the same fitting-only protocol and target mapping. Scaled column positions are still tabular columns, not timestamps. Unknown-category diagnostics, zero-preserving transformations and saved names must travel with every model.')
code(c,"finish(s, findings='Systematic fitting EDA exposes class imbalance, repeated patterns, zero-heavy fields and numeric tails. Fresh train-only preprocessing exactly reproduces the prior six matrices; round trips and cross-role overlap checks pass.', decisions='Keep the raw-numeric tree and log/robust-scaled representations. Preserve all 42 source predictors, protocol markers, nonbinary FTP values, zeros and extremes. Keep categorical groups intact.', limitations='The extractor and timing semantics are not independently verified. Scaling does not remove outliers, create information or establish performance. Unknown categories lack learned category-specific effects; rare-class support remains small.', next_question='Can simple count summaries make recorded information easier for models to use?')")
save('03',c)

c=notebook('04','Create three understandable flow features','**Our question:** Can a small number of meaningful formulas make existing flow information easier for a model to use?\n\n**Inputs:** Fitting rows and four measured counters: source/destination bytes and packets. Earlier preprocessing and frozen roles remain fixed.\n\n**Expected result:** Three explicitly defined features, candidate/rejection evidence and verified formulas in every development role. These features add representations—not new measured information. No model training occurs here.')
md(c,'## 2. Explain the formulas\n\n**Question:** What exactly are we adding?\n\n**Why:** A feature must have a formula, meaning and prediction-time scope.\n\n**Method:** `fe_total_bytes = sbytes + dbytes` measures total recorded bidirectional volume in bytes. `fe_byte_balance = (sbytes - dbytes)/(sbytes + dbytes)` describes direction: +1 source-only, −1 destination-only, 0 equal volume. Packet balance applies the same idea to packet counts. Source/destination do not necessarily mean client/server.\n\nWhen a denominator is zero, use 0 as a documented sentinel. This makes no-flow and perfectly balanced flow share the balance value, but original counts remain available to distinguish them. The formulas require completed-flow counters; early-stream or real-time availability is not established. The proposal table also shows alternatives and their redundancy questions.')
md(c,'## 3. Work examples and examine fitting evidence\n\n**Question:** Do the formulas behave sensibly, and are they distinct enough to test?\n\n**Why:** A bounded ratio can expose an interaction to a linear model, while a tree may already learn it from counts.\n\n**Method:** Start with four worked flows, including zero traffic. Then inspect fitting distributions, zeros, extremes and class support. Compare both balance correlations by rows and by distinct original patterns. Check bytes/packet against existing `smean`/`dmean` and packet rate against `rate`. Labels describe associations only; they never enter a formula.')
code(c,'audit.engineering(s)')
md(c,'### Check denominators, extremes and existing rate/size fields\n\nA formula can be numerically safe and still be redundant. We inspect zero-denominator support, real extreme examples and the protected earlier tolerance checks. The next figure puts original counter summaries beside the engineered direction summaries; it changes the representation, not the measured information. The rate diagnostic with one fewer packet often matches `rate` closely, but an inferred approximation is not a verified extractor definition.')
code(c,'audit.engineering_detail(s)')
md(c,'## 4. Read the result, not just the correlation\n\nHigh byte/packet correlation says these summaries often move together. It does not show that either improves detection, nor that one is always replaceable by the other. Packet balance ignores packet size; byte balance reflects it. Total bytes is an explicit sum. The before/after comparison is the original directional counts versus their volume and bounded-direction summaries. All original values remain present.')
code(c,"from src.data.feature_engineering import safe_ratio\nflow = s['fit']\nbyte_total = flow.sbytes + flow.dbytes\npacket_total = flow.spkts + flow.dpkts\nfeatures = pd.DataFrame({\n    'fe_total_bytes': byte_total,\n    'fe_byte_balance': safe_ratio(flow.sbytes - flow.dbytes, byte_total),\n    'fe_packet_balance': safe_ratio(flow.spkts - flow.dpkts, packet_total),\n})\nassert np.array_equal(features.to_numpy(), audit.retained_features(flow).to_numpy())\ndisplay(pd.concat([flow[['sbytes','dbytes','spkts','dpkts']], features], axis=1).head())\nprint('These are the three actual formulas. No label or fitted parameter is used.')")
md(c,'## 5. Fix the small candidate set\n\n**Decision:** Keep total bytes, byte balance and packet balance for matched model experiments. Reject extra bytes-per-packet features because the dataset already has `smean` and `dmean`, with small differences consistent with rounding. Reject a new average packet-rate formula from this small set: `rate` already represents a rate and its exact extractor convention is unresolved.\n\n**Uncertainty:** Retained does not mean proven useful. The decision is a small, interpretable experiment contract. The next notebooks isolate each engineered contribution while preserving original42 and all45 references.')
md(c,'## 6. Apply exactly the same formulas\n\n**Question:** Can we reproduce each saved engineered input?\n\n**Why:** Formula drift or row misalignment would invalidate later ablations.\n\n**Method:** Compute the stateless formulas in every existing role, verify equality to earlier saved values, save with round-trip float precision, reload and compare. Preserve role links. Re-audit exact overlap in the full augmented scaled/tree representations. The learned engineered scaler is the protected earlier train-fitted artifact; calibration is transformed for integrity only.')
code(c,'audit.engineering_verify(s)')
md(c,'## 7. What a later model comparison must show\n\nA useful engineered representation must improve a matched model against original42 without quietly changing rows, settings or metrics. Test total volume first, each balance after volume, and the second balance after the first. Look at ten-class macro F1 and class-level tradeoffs; a change driven by one or two rare-class rows is uncertain. A formula can help one family and hurt another.')
code(c,"finish(s, findings='Three transparent completed-flow summaries reproduce the earlier engineered inputs. Byte and packet balances are strongly related but not identical. Extra packet-size/rate ideas overlap existing measurements.', decisions='Retain total bytes, byte balance and packet balance as candidates; preserve all 42 original predictors and both preprocessing baselines. Use the same zero sentinel and formulas in every role.', limitations='No new measurements, botnet ground truth, DNS transactions or time windows were created. Descriptive target association is not evidence of model improvement. The zero sentinel needs original counts for interpretation.', next_question='Which focused combinations should be carried forward, with redundancy and stability evidence?')")
save('04',c)

c=notebook('05','Choose fair feature comparisons','**Our question:** Which clearly defined combinations should later baseline models compare?\n\n**Inputs:** Original42, the three engineered summaries, frozen roles, fitting evidence and protected row-linked representations.\n\n**Expected result:** A five-set experiment shortlist. This is not a final selection of the best original predictors and not a claim that engineered features improve detection.')
md(c,'## 2. Declare the five comparisons\n\n**Question:** Which experiment isolates each engineered idea?\n\n**Why:** Too many arbitrary subsets create search optimism and make conclusions hard to explain.\n\n**Method:** Preserve original42 (reference), total_only43 (volume), total_and_byte44 (volume + byte direction), total_and_packet44 (volume + packet direction), all45 (all additions). Source-name definitions keep every original predictor and complete one-hot categorical groups. Compare a 44-set with total_only43 for one balance’s contribution; compare all45 with each 44-set for the second balance. Every set has a specific question, so none is removed just because the balances correlate.')
code(c,"definitions = audit.read_json(ROOT/'models/preprocessing/05/05_comparison_sets.json')['sets']\ndisplay(pd.DataFrame([dict(set=k,features=len(definitions[k]['retained']),omitted=', '.join(definitions[k]['omitted']),question=definitions[k]['reason']) for k in audit.SETS]))")
md(c,'## 3. Check redundant and low-variation inputs\n\n**Question:** Are some columns copies or almost constant?\n\n**Why:** Redundancy can affect model behaviour, but a small rare protocol subgroup can still matter.\n\n**Method:** On fitting rows only, compare exact numeric equality, Spearman correlations and dominant-value fractions. Report minority rows and distinct-pattern support. A 99.5% dominant-value flag prompts inspection; it is not an automatic deletion rule. Both FTP fields match in fitting data but have different intended meanings and previously audited mismatches across supplied files.')
code(c,'audit.selection_evidence(s)')
md(c,'## 4. Assess associations with grouped folds\n\n**Question:** Do descriptive feature/target relationships persist when patterns are held together?\n\n**Why:** Repeated records can inflate row-based associations.\n\n**Method:** Reuse the three saved grouped folds inside fitting only. For numeric features, fit eight-bin quantile edges on each inner-training fold, then measure held-out association. Cramér’s V summarizes dependence between feature levels and class labels (0 weak, 1 strong under this table). Compare row-based scores with scores giving one original pattern total weight one. Preserve conflicting label versions. This is not a model or proof of useful feature selection. A constant observed binned feature has an undefined score; do not replace it with invented zero relevance.')
code(c,'audit.association_stability(s)')
md(c,'## 5. Make the shortlist and verify every input\n\n**Question:** Do the actual saved matrices implement those five source-name contracts?\n\n**Why:** One-hot columns must travel as whole groups, with exactly the expected rows and labels.\n\n**Method:** Verify feature order, finite values, source-to-encoded mappings, raw CSV values and frozen row links. Compare every candidate matrix to its exact slice of the protected augmented input. Recompute cross-role exact matches for all five tree/scaled variants. Removing a column could merge previously different rows; any matches must be disclosed, not silently fixed by resplitting. Save a hash-bound input index pointing to preserved role-linked files rather than duplicate hundreds of megabytes.')
code(c,'audit.shortlist(s)')
md(c,'## 6. Be precise about “selection”\n\n**Decision:** Carry five interpretable representation variants into Notebook 06. Keep all original42 features, including both FTP fields. No learned predictive selector is fitted, and no final subset or winning balance is declared. A stronger selector would need a specific research purpose, fitting-only group-aware validation and a later fair model comparison; merely sorting an association table is insufficient.\n\n**Uncertainty:** The association estimates are sensitive to bins, repeats, conflicts and rare-class support. Categorical blocks can have many levels. Model improvement must be demonstrated by matched experiments, not rankings.')
code(c,"finish(s, findings='Fitting redundancy and three-fold descriptive association calculations reproduce the earlier evidence. FTP fields are fitting copies; balances are related. All five role-linked candidate representations verify and have zero cross-role exact matches.', decisions='Shortlist original42, total_only43, total_and_byte44, total_and_packet44 and all45. Keep whole categorical groups and every original predictor. Fit no predictive selector and claim no winner.', limitations='This is a representation shortlist, not original-feature subset discovery. Rankings, correlation and low variation do not establish improved detection. Grouping recorded patterns does not establish host/time independence.', next_question='Do matched simple and stronger tabular models benefit from any engineered representation under the frozen development protocol?')")
save('05',c)

c=notebook('06','Test features against credible baselines','**Our question:** Do the three engineered summaries improve intrusion detection, and what performance bar should later Conv1D/attention models face?\n\n**Inputs:** The five predeclared feature sets and frozen development roles.\n\n**Expected result:** Matched unweighted linear, single-tree and stronger boosted-tree results, fitting-only grouped tuning, a provisional development candidate and honest class-level limitations. No confidence calibration, threshold selection, reserved-role refitting or official-test evaluation.')
md(c,'## 2. Understand the earlier experiment\n\nThe preserved Notebook 06 compared ten fixed fits: logistic and a single tree across five sets. Those are useful controls, not the strongest available tabular benchmarks. The single tree with original42 reached macro F1 about .6093, while logistic was around .42. All logistic sets missed every Worms row. These are historical results on the same model-selection role, so the role is already exposed. We will retain every earlier artifact and distinguish fresh bounded-tuning results from those controls.')
code(c,"show_prior(s,'06_model_selection_metrics',10)")
md(c,'## 3. Write the plan before new fitting\n\n**Question:** How can we add a credible stronger benchmark without uncontrolled searching?\n\n**Why:** A neural model should face strong tabular alternatives, and feature effects should not be confounded with different settings.\n\n**Plan:** Use regularized one-versus-rest logistic regression on scaled sparse inputs; a bounded-depth decision tree on tree inputs; and LightGBM, a boosted-tree ensemble that iteratively combines many small trees, on the same tree inputs. LightGBM is available in the project environment and accepts these sparse matrices. All models are unweighted. No class-weight search is mixed into the primary experiment.\n\nTune only **two predeclared configurations per family**, on original42 and the same three grouped fitting folds (18 inner fits). Refit preprocessing inside each inner-training fold. Freeze one setting per family, then apply that exact setting across the five feature variants. Thus feature-specific search effort is equally zero. The search is modest, not exhaustive. Fix seed 42 and 120 boosting rounds; do not use selection rows for early stopping. A verified earlier final fit may be reused only when settings match exactly.\n\n**Choice rules:** Inner mean fixed-ten-class macro F1 selects settings; within .001 prefer the first, lower-capacity declared setting. Final provisional choice uses macro F1 on model-selection; within .001 prefer fewer source features, then logistic, tree, ensemble, then higher score and declared set order. This is a practical preference, not a significance test. Nonconverged models remain visible but cannot be selected.')
code(c,"from src.research_revision import baselines as baseline\nbaseline.declare(s)")
md(c,'## 4. Tune inside fitting only\n\n**Question:** Which bounded setting generalizes best to held-out fitting patterns?\n\n**Why:** Full-fitting scalers or vocabularies would expose inner holdout distributions during tuning.\n\n**Method:** Use the protected fold assignments. Identical original patterns stay together, even with conflicting labels. Clone and refit the original preprocessing on each fold’s inner training rows, then predict that fold’s holdout. Save each fold score, support, timing and preprocessing provenance. Checkpoint fingerprints prevent an interrupted run from silently changing the search. Fold ranges are descriptive variation, not confidence intervals.')
code(c,'baseline.tuning(s)')
md(c,'## 5. Fit the matched candidates\n\n**Question:** What happens when each family sees exactly the same fitting rows under each representation?\n\n**Why:** The engineered feature question is a within-model comparison.\n\n**Method:** Fit or verify-reuse one train-only model per family/set, save exact settings, reload the model and reproduce predictions. Predict only `model_selection`. Class order is fixed to ten classes. IDs, targets, group IDs, positions and split metadata are never matrix inputs. Calibration and test receive no model predictions.')
code(c,'baseline.experiments(s)')
md(c,'## 6. Read the metrics correctly\n\n**Macro F1** averages ten class F1 values equally. Each F1 balances precision (correct among predicted cases) and recall (found among actual cases). **Weighted F1** weights class F1 by support, so common classes dominate more. Accuracy counts all correct rows.\n\nFor Attack/Normal, a non-Normal multiclass prediction is Attack. **Attack recall** is found attacks / all actual attacks. **Attack precision** is actual attacks / all predicted attacks. **False-positive rate** is Normal rows incorrectly called Attack / all Normal rows. These binary numbers can look strong even when an attack is assigned the wrong attack category.\n\nThe attack score `1 − P(Normal)` supports a threshold-independent precision–recall curve and **average precision** (AP), which summarizes precision across recall levels. Raw probabilities are not calibrated confidence. We do not pick an operational threshold here. A confusion-matrix row contains one actual class; the normalized row shows fractions of that class.')
